WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.940
Social media is flipping out over the Spanish
sci-fi/horror flick The Platform, which secured

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.000
its stateside release through Netflix.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.269
The film is being called a perfect parable
for our troubled times, not to mention a taut,

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:13.450
unblinking, merciless thriller.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.560
In recent times, Netflix has been regularly
shouldering its way into the national spotlight

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.720
with the kind of original content that seems
specifically calibrated to grab the eyeballs

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.720
of millions of people all at once.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.939
First, on February 13th, there was Love is
Blind, an unapologetically melodramatic reality

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:33.200
series focused on a batch of couples who date,
court, and make the decision as to whether

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.700
or not to get married without ever actually,
you know, seeing each other.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.380
Then, on March 20th, there was Tiger King,
an original docu-series focused on private

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:46.700
zoo owner Joe Exotic and a cast of insane
caricatures, all of whom are, improbably,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.700
real people.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.020
"When Joe rented that helicopter, it gave
him a lot of really bad ideas."

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.420
Now, there's The Platform, which as of the
making of this video has earned an 82% Fresh

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.440
rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.910
The show has viewers in disbelief over how
ridiculously timely it is - not to mention

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.159
well-crafted, well-acted, and relentlessly
disturbing.

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:10.840
American audiences aren't likely to recognize
much of the cast, but that will only lend

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.200
even more gritty authenticity to the proceedings.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.270
So, what is The Platform all about, anyway?

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.290
The movie centers on Goreng, a resident of
the Pit, which is a prison constructed like

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.479
a tower of indeterminate height.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:26.479
Each level has massive holes in its floor
and ceiling, through which the titular platform

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:27.920
descends every day.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.569
On this platform is enough food for all of
the Pit's prisoners - but the inmates near

00:01:31.569 --> 00:01:35.700
the top are free to take as much food as they
want, often not leaving enough - or any at

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.950
all - for those on the lower levels.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.530
Throughout the film, Goreng finds himself
assigned to different levels of the Pit, sharing

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.979
space with some unsettling roommates.

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.240
Also figuring heavily into the movie's increasingly
alarming narrative: a mysterious woman who

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.069
rides the platform, searching the Pit's various
levels for her child.

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:56.179
Of course, Goreng is eventually confronted
with violence, cannibalism, and other unspeakable

00:01:56.179 --> 00:02:00.610
situations - all while trying to figure out
just how far down the levels go, and attempting

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.390
to get the prisoners to cooperate rather than
working against each other.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.119
The film sports a bizarre final reveal that
will leave you scratching your head - and

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.060
wondering just what kind of message we should
be sending to those on the top levels of our

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.660
own (metaphorical) Pit.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.610
It's easy to view The Platform as an allegory
for class warfare, an issue which has gained

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:21.100
more and more political prominence over the
course of the last decade or so.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.970
On its surface, it can also be seen as a simple,
stark message: Things could be a whole lot

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:26.970
worse!

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.360
This seemed to be the takeaway for several
Twitterers, including user @HansGr00ber, who

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.640
wrote with tongue planted firmly in cheek,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.970
"If you want to watch a grim film about being
confined in a nightmare prison without food

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.610
I suggest watching The Platform on Netflix.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.100
It's pretty good."

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.780
User @katevaldez was a little more succinct,
writing,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.810
"Crazy s---.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.820
Food will never be the same again."

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.100
User @kathdeclaw posted a strong recommendation,
along with an equally strong warning.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.880
"Just finished watching [The Platform on]
Netflix and I'm wrecked and...I need to lie

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.880
down.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.940
You know it's a good one when it's that impactful.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.620
It was extreme and hard to watch but has a
relevant message apt in these desperate times.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.960
12/10 highly recommended."

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.900
User @steph_bonchi echoed the sentiment.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.270
"I can't believe The Platform was only 1hr
35 minutes.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:14.660
It felt like 2 - 3 hours simply because of
how heavy, gruesome, irky and painful it is

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.660
to watch.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.070
It was torture and I couldn't blink for fear
that I would miss a second."

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.991
The Platform is strong stuff, indeed - but
for fans of jolting horror, it should be a

00:03:22.991 --> 00:03:23.991
welcome escape.

00:03:23.991 --> 00:03:27.400
A gory, nerve-wrenching, really messed-up
escape.

